1
00:00:00,525 --> 00:00:01,918
Does your cat actually love you?

2
00:00:02,275 --> 00:00:03,645
Or are you just the person who

3
00:00:03,568 --> 00:00:04,616
opens the food?

4
00:00:04,997 --> 00:00:06,691
It is one of the oldest questions

5
00:00:06,764 --> 00:00:08,357
every cat owner asks.

6
00:00:09,437 --> 00:00:10,689
Dogs make it obvious.

7
00:00:11,066 --> 00:00:12,938
They jump, they wag, they lick your

8
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:13,499
face.

9
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:16,284
Cats are quieter, and that makes people

10
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,212
think they are cold.

11
00:00:18,241 --> 00:00:20,390
But scientists have been studying the bond

12
00:00:20,337 --> 00:00:23,273
between cats and humans, and the answer

13
00:00:23,211 --> 00:00:25,665
is a lot sweeter than the stereotype.

14
00:00:26,704 --> 00:00:28,984
Chapter one: the cold cat myth.

15
00:00:29,342 --> 00:00:31,870
The idea that cats are aloof comes

16
00:00:31,801 --> 00:00:33,219
partly from their history.

17
00:00:34,272 --> 00:00:36,355
The wild ancestor of the house cat,

18
00:00:36,513 --> 00:00:40,224
the African wildcat, lives and hunts mostly

19
00:00:40,162 --> 00:00:40,657
alone.

20
00:00:41,022 --> 00:00:43,776
No pack, no team, no boss.

21
00:00:44,870 --> 00:00:47,189
Dogs come from wolves, which live in

22
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:49,639
families and read each other constantly.

23
00:00:50,015 --> 00:00:52,794
So dogs show feelings in big, loud

24
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:53,365
ways.

25
00:00:54,453 --> 00:00:58,047
Cats show affection in small, quiet signals.

26
00:00:58,421 --> 00:01:00,342
If you don't know the signals, it

27
00:01:00,234 --> 00:01:01,739
looks like nothing is happening.

28
00:01:02,758 --> 00:01:05,838
And here is the surprising part: domestic

29
00:01:05,767 --> 00:01:08,074
cats are far more social than their

30
00:01:07,996 --> 00:01:09,264
wild ancestors.

31
00:01:09,610 --> 00:01:11,113
Living with people changed them.

32
00:01:12,126 --> 00:01:15,293
Many house cats form close friendships with

33
00:01:15,209 --> 00:01:17,525
other cats, and with the humans they

34
00:01:17,427 --> 00:01:18,198
live with.

35
00:01:18,549 --> 00:01:19,488
They just do it on their own

36
00:01:19,446 --> 00:01:20,162
terms.

37
00:01:21,242 --> 00:01:23,754
Cats also recognize their owner's voice.

38
00:01:24,166 --> 00:01:26,993
In one study, cats reacted more to

39
00:01:26,926 --> 00:01:29,190
a recording of their owner calling their

40
00:01:29,117 --> 00:01:30,843
name than to strangers.

41
00:01:31,192 --> 00:01:33,382
They may not run over, but their

42
00:01:33,451 --> 00:01:35,762
ears and head turn toward you.

43
00:01:36,788 --> 00:01:39,240
Chapter two: the attachment test.

44
00:01:39,701 --> 00:01:43,554
In 2019, researchers at Oregon State University

45
00:01:43,882 --> 00:01:45,779
ran a famous experiment.

46
00:01:46,785 --> 00:01:49,347
They used a test originally designed for

47
00:01:49,253 --> 00:01:50,263
human babies.

48
00:01:50,634 --> 00:01:52,696
The owner and the cat sit together

49
00:01:52,632 --> 00:01:54,485
in a strange room for a few

50
00:01:54,428 --> 00:01:55,055
minutes.

51
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:57,897
Then the owner leaves for two minutes,

52
00:01:58,043 --> 00:01:59,329
and comes back.

53
00:01:59,726 --> 00:02:01,811
The scientists watch what the cat does

54
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:02,520
at the reunion.

55
00:02:03,579 --> 00:02:06,429
A securely attached cat greets the owner,

56
00:02:06,815 --> 00:02:09,763
then relaxes and goes back to exploring.

57
00:02:10,117 --> 00:02:12,097
The owner is its safe base.

58
00:02:13,155 --> 00:02:16,270
An insecure cat either clings too much,

59
00:02:16,683 --> 00:02:19,727
or avoids the owner completely, even after

60
00:02:19,627 --> 00:02:21,691
being alone in a strange place.

61
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:23,401
The result?

62
00:02:23,765 --> 00:02:25,691
About sixty four percent of the cats

63
00:02:25,631 --> 00:02:28,052
were securely attached to their owners.

64
00:02:29,088 --> 00:02:31,754
That is almost exactly the same percentage

65
00:02:31,699 --> 00:02:34,624
found in human babies, and very close

66
00:02:34,529 --> 00:02:35,338
to dogs.

67
00:02:36,426 --> 00:02:38,545
So most cats don't just tolerate us.

68
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:40,788
They use us as a source of

69
00:02:40,716 --> 00:02:43,842
comfort and security, just like a child

70
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:44,790
uses a parent.

71
00:02:45,805 --> 00:02:48,199
Chapter three: the slow blink.

72
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:49,810
If you have ever seen your cat

73
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:51,893
look at you and slowly close its

74
00:02:51,842 --> 00:02:54,406
eyes, you have received a compliment.

75
00:02:55,418 --> 00:02:57,986
In the wild, staring is a threat.

76
00:02:58,368 --> 00:03:00,037
Closing your eyes in front of someone

77
00:02:59,979 --> 00:03:01,850
means: I trust you.

78
00:03:02,224 --> 00:03:03,501
I don't feel in danger.

79
00:03:04,590 --> 00:03:07,996
In 2020, psychologists in the United Kingdom

80
00:03:08,092 --> 00:03:09,114
tested this.

81
00:03:09,495 --> 00:03:11,855
Owners slow blinked at their cats, and

82
00:03:11,733 --> 00:03:13,614
the cats slow blinked back.

83
00:03:14,653 --> 00:03:16,062
Then a stranger tried it.

84
00:03:16,441 --> 00:03:18,183
Cats were more likely to approach a

85
00:03:18,121 --> 00:03:20,800
stranger who slow blinked at them than

86
00:03:20,709 --> 00:03:22,385
one who kept a neutral face.

87
00:03:23,477 --> 00:03:26,409
Scientists sometimes call the slow blink the

88
00:03:26,333 --> 00:03:27,261
cat smile.

89
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:29,023
And yes, you can do it too:

90
00:03:29,530 --> 00:03:31,834
look at your cat, narrow your eyes,

91
00:03:32,005 --> 00:03:33,223
and blink slowly.

92
00:03:34,269 --> 00:03:37,132
If your cat blinks back, congratulations.

93
00:03:37,473 --> 00:03:39,190
You just had a real conversation in

94
00:03:39,115 --> 00:03:40,080
cat language.

95
00:03:41,072 --> 00:03:43,738
Chapter four: headbutts and rubbing.

96
00:03:44,067 --> 00:03:45,899
When your cat bumps its head into

97
00:03:45,824 --> 00:03:48,514
you, it has a name: bunting.

98
00:03:49,546 --> 00:03:51,730
Cats have scent glands on their cheeks,

99
00:03:51,931 --> 00:03:53,413
chin and forehead.

100
00:03:53,771 --> 00:03:56,045
When they rub against you, they leave

101
00:03:55,971 --> 00:03:57,115
their scent on you.

102
00:03:58,154 --> 00:04:00,896
Cats that live together do the same

103
00:04:00,894 --> 00:04:02,305
thing to each other.

104
00:04:02,667 --> 00:04:04,605
It creates a shared group smell that

105
00:04:04,554 --> 00:04:06,506
says: we are family.

106
00:04:07,539 --> 00:04:08,770
So when your cat rubs on your

107
00:04:08,709 --> 00:04:11,408
legs, it is basically marking you as

108
00:04:11,336 --> 00:04:12,247
part of its group.

109
00:04:12,615 --> 00:04:13,809
You belong to the cat.

110
00:04:14,836 --> 00:04:16,321
Licking works the same way.

111
00:04:16,707 --> 00:04:19,085
Friendly cats groom each other, mostly on

112
00:04:18,967 --> 00:04:20,305
the head and neck.

113
00:04:20,678 --> 00:04:22,339
It is called allogrooming.

114
00:04:23,369 --> 00:04:24,921
If your cat licks your hand or

115
00:04:24,832 --> 00:04:26,920
your hair, it is treating you like

116
00:04:26,817 --> 00:04:27,884
a close friend.

117
00:04:28,268 --> 00:04:31,200
A slightly rough, sandpaper kind of friend.

118
00:04:32,237 --> 00:04:34,987
Chapter five: kneading and purring.

119
00:04:35,350 --> 00:04:37,285
Kneading is when your cat pushes its

120
00:04:37,224 --> 00:04:39,318
paws in and out on a soft

121
00:04:39,275 --> 00:04:40,965
blanket, or on you.

122
00:04:41,997 --> 00:04:44,366
Kittens knead their mother's belly to get

123
00:04:44,301 --> 00:04:45,284
milk flowing.

124
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:47,711
Many adult cats keep doing it when

125
00:04:47,603 --> 00:04:49,722
they feel safe and happy.

126
00:04:50,793 --> 00:04:52,612
So when your cat kneads your lap,

127
00:04:52,873 --> 00:04:54,402
it is going back to the most

128
00:04:54,317 --> 00:04:57,021
comfortable feeling it has ever known.

129
00:04:58,066 --> 00:04:59,998
Purring also starts in kittenhood.

130
00:05:00,341 --> 00:05:02,953
Kittens purr while nursing, and the mother

131
00:05:02,891 --> 00:05:03,915
purrs back.

132
00:05:04,308 --> 00:05:06,009
It is a signal of closeness.

133
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:10,080
Adult cats mostly purr around the people

134
00:05:09,968 --> 00:05:10,778
they trust.

135
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:12,876
But purring can also happen when a

136
00:05:12,814 --> 00:05:15,831
cat is stressed or in pain, so

137
00:05:15,752 --> 00:05:17,081
watch the rest of the body.

138
00:05:18,118 --> 00:05:21,075
A purr with relaxed eyes, soft paws

139
00:05:21,033 --> 00:05:22,737
and a slow tail is one of

140
00:05:22,615 --> 00:05:25,215
the clearest signs of a happy, bonded

141
00:05:25,176 --> 00:05:25,640
cat.

142
00:05:26,690 --> 00:05:29,440
Chapter six: tail up and the welcome

143
00:05:29,372 --> 00:05:29,772
home.

144
00:05:30,136 --> 00:05:31,904
A cat walking toward you with its

145
00:05:31,816 --> 00:05:34,057
tail straight up is saying hello.

146
00:05:35,096 --> 00:05:38,120
Researchers found that cats raise their tails

147
00:05:38,057 --> 00:05:40,891
when greeting friends, and they rarely do

148
00:05:40,838 --> 00:05:42,686
it with cats they don't like.

149
00:05:43,752 --> 00:05:45,000
A tail up with a little hook

150
00:05:44,895 --> 00:05:47,329
at the tip is extra friendly.

151
00:05:47,683 --> 00:05:49,830
It is the cat version of waving

152
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:51,069
at you from across the room.

153
00:05:52,071 --> 00:05:54,630
In a Swedish study, cats left alone

154
00:05:54,568 --> 00:05:57,110
for four hours greeted their owners with

155
00:05:57,028 --> 00:05:59,955
more purring and more stretching than cats

156
00:05:59,917 --> 00:06:01,899
left alone for just half an hour.

157
00:06:02,925 --> 00:06:05,309
In other words, cats do notice when

158
00:06:05,196 --> 00:06:07,058
you are gone, and many of them

159
00:06:06,941 --> 00:06:08,582
are happy when you come back.

160
00:06:09,628 --> 00:06:11,475
Some cats even wait by the door,

161
00:06:11,674 --> 00:06:13,080
because they have learned the sound of

162
00:06:12,958 --> 00:06:14,820
your car or your keys.

163
00:06:15,219 --> 00:06:16,774
That is not a coincidence.

164
00:06:17,796 --> 00:06:21,836
Chapter seven: gifts and following you everywhere.

165
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:24,963
Cats that go outside sometimes bring home

166
00:06:24,896 --> 00:06:27,733
a gift: a mouse, a bird, a

167
00:06:27,705 --> 00:06:28,334
leaf.

168
00:06:29,403 --> 00:06:31,173
Nobody knows for sure why they do

169
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:31,675
this.

170
00:06:32,038 --> 00:06:33,757
They might be bringing prey to a

171
00:06:33,701 --> 00:06:36,368
safe place, or sharing with their group.

172
00:06:37,350 --> 00:06:39,481
Either way, you are included in that

173
00:06:39,394 --> 00:06:39,833
group.

174
00:06:40,225 --> 00:06:41,770
Gross, but kind of sweet.

175
00:06:42,792 --> 00:06:43,931
Then there is the bathroom.

176
00:06:44,291 --> 00:06:46,365
Many cats follow their owners from room

177
00:06:46,281 --> 00:06:49,746
to room, and absolutely insist on joining

178
00:06:49,661 --> 00:06:50,655
them in the bathroom.

179
00:06:51,665 --> 00:06:54,093
Part of it is curiosity: a closed

180
00:06:54,042 --> 00:06:55,684
door is a mystery.

181
00:06:56,066 --> 00:06:57,741
But part of it is simply wanting

182
00:06:57,634 --> 00:06:58,822
to be where you are.

183
00:06:59,868 --> 00:07:01,839
Sleeping on you is another big one.

184
00:07:02,212 --> 00:07:04,414
A sleeping cat is at its most

185
00:07:04,353 --> 00:07:06,759
vulnerable, and it chose to do that

186
00:07:06,671 --> 00:07:07,613
on top of you.

187
00:07:08,636 --> 00:07:10,768
Showing the belly is similar.

188
00:07:11,139 --> 00:07:13,159
It is a sign of deep trust,

189
00:07:13,375 --> 00:07:15,303
but usually not an invitation for a

190
00:07:15,216 --> 00:07:15,990
belly rub.

191
00:07:16,370 --> 00:07:17,403
Look, don't touch.

192
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:20,708
Some cats also bring you their favorite

193
00:07:20,678 --> 00:07:22,976
toy, drop it at your feet, and

194
00:07:22,899 --> 00:07:23,504
stare.

195
00:07:23,868 --> 00:07:25,858
That is usually an invitation to play,

196
00:07:26,358 --> 00:07:27,964
and a sign they see you as

197
00:07:27,863 --> 00:07:28,954
a real playmate.

198
00:07:29,973 --> 00:07:32,210
Chapter eight: the love hormone.

199
00:07:32,611 --> 00:07:35,900
Oxytocin is a hormone linked to bonding

200
00:07:35,812 --> 00:07:38,382
in humans and many other animals.

201
00:07:39,385 --> 00:07:41,819
In a 2016 experiment for a TV

202
00:07:41,801 --> 00:07:45,467
documentary, dog owners' dogs showed a big

203
00:07:45,424 --> 00:07:48,111
rise in oxytocin after playing with them.

204
00:07:48,479 --> 00:07:50,651
Cats showed a much smaller rise.

205
00:07:51,702 --> 00:07:54,495
Headlines said dogs love us five times

206
00:07:54,431 --> 00:07:55,521
more than cats.

207
00:07:55,916 --> 00:07:58,154
But it was a tiny study, and

208
00:07:58,376 --> 00:08:01,623
cats were tested in a stressful, unfamiliar

209
00:08:01,594 --> 00:08:02,300
place.

210
00:08:03,364 --> 00:08:07,217
Newer research from Japan suggests something interesting:

211
00:08:07,685 --> 00:08:10,350
a cat's oxytocin goes up when contact

212
00:08:10,293 --> 00:08:11,908
happens on the cat's terms.

213
00:08:12,921 --> 00:08:14,519
When the cat chooses to come to

214
00:08:14,439 --> 00:08:16,879
you, the bond hormone rises.

215
00:08:17,227 --> 00:08:19,284
When it is grabbed and held against

216
00:08:19,196 --> 00:08:20,812
its will, it doesn't.

217
00:08:21,863 --> 00:08:23,749
So cat love is real.

218
00:08:24,106 --> 00:08:25,643
It is just consent based.

219
00:08:26,028 --> 00:08:27,727
The cat wants to decide when the

220
00:08:27,612 --> 00:08:28,635
cuddle happens.

221
00:08:29,669 --> 00:08:32,077
Chapter nine: how to get more love

222
00:08:31,995 --> 00:08:32,758
from your cat.

223
00:08:33,262 --> 00:08:35,170
First, let the cat come to you.

224
00:08:35,522 --> 00:08:36,786
Offer a hand and wait.

225
00:08:37,811 --> 00:08:40,194
Cats who are allowed to choose, and

226
00:08:40,094 --> 00:08:42,951
to leave whenever they want, actually stay

227
00:08:42,890 --> 00:08:45,383
longer and come back more often.

228
00:08:46,439 --> 00:08:49,668
Second, pet the right places: the cheeks,

229
00:08:49,604 --> 00:08:51,130
the chin, and the base of the

230
00:08:51,081 --> 00:08:51,700
ears.

231
00:08:52,097 --> 00:08:53,914
Most cats are less keen on the

232
00:08:53,850 --> 00:08:55,276
belly and the tail.

233
00:08:56,370 --> 00:08:57,719
Third, play every day.

234
00:08:58,103 --> 00:08:59,932
A short hunting game with a wand

235
00:08:59,890 --> 00:09:03,371
toy builds trust, burns energy, and makes

236
00:09:03,254 --> 00:09:04,813
you the most fun thing in the

237
00:09:04,749 --> 00:09:05,399
house.

238
00:09:06,516 --> 00:09:07,846
Fourth, keep a routine.

239
00:09:08,222 --> 00:09:10,143
Cats love predictability.

240
00:09:10,547 --> 00:09:13,456
Meals, play and quiet time at the

241
00:09:13,382 --> 00:09:15,861
same hours make them feel safe.

242
00:09:17,004 --> 00:09:19,686
Fifth, give your cat high places and

243
00:09:19,582 --> 00:09:20,701
hiding spots.

244
00:09:21,091 --> 00:09:23,026
A cat that feels safe in its

245
00:09:22,968 --> 00:09:26,178
home has a lot more energy left

246
00:09:26,110 --> 00:09:26,960
for affection.

247
00:09:27,968 --> 00:09:30,654
And finally, talk to your cat and

248
00:09:30,583 --> 00:09:31,730
slow blink at it.

249
00:09:32,078 --> 00:09:34,716
They recognize your voice, and they really

250
00:09:34,616 --> 00:09:35,423
are listening.

251
00:09:36,468 --> 00:09:37,884
So, does your cat love you?

252
00:09:38,251 --> 00:09:41,623
Science says most cats form real, secure

253
00:09:41,562 --> 00:09:44,253
bonds with their humans, just like babies

254
00:09:44,153 --> 00:09:45,024
and dogs do.

255
00:09:46,086 --> 00:09:48,061
They just say it differently: with a

256
00:09:48,001 --> 00:09:51,078
slow blink, a head bump, a raised

257
00:09:51,026 --> 00:09:53,568
tail, a purr on your lap, or

258
00:09:53,494 --> 00:09:55,102
a dead mouse on your doormat.

259
00:09:56,123 --> 00:09:57,584
How does your cat show you love?

260
00:09:57,950 --> 00:09:59,914
Tell me in the comments, and I'll

261
00:09:59,845 --> 00:10:01,475
see you in the next video.
